                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Going by whats in game some people see this as a self-loathing artist simulator by way of that 14 year old theater kid who thought he was destined to make art in all things and who's very life and calling is dependent on the expression of the intangible and other shit those kinda kids say that makes people cringe - even the people who love them.\n\nSome other people see this as a truly heartfelt, earnest, sincere attempt at putting a lot of what is inside the creator out there for everyone to see. with some of it being easy to parse, other parts, not so much. with some parts being playful and fun, lightweight and silly, and others being uncanny, odd, surreal, unsettling, and at least in one scene, pretty relatable, inspiring, and depending on your life experience, heartbreaking.\n\nSo my take, as i'm sure others have to, is that this is actually both of those things at the exact same time in equal measure, which creates something that in a way is a lot more human, real, and efficiently done then a lot of more subtle self-referential artistic content on steam, let alone dream simulators. for that and the fact that its free, punchy, ran well for me anyway, and didn't overstay its welcome (tho ymmv there), even if i agree with both sides and even though im kinda cringing while also tearing up a bit i admire the courage on display, i admire the output, and im glad i played it and if you're curious you should try it.\n\nnice work dude! keep doin you.",
